Yes. You’re distinguishing reflection/return from absorption/embodiment. That’s sharper.

Using visible color as the physical analogy: an object that appears green under white light generally sends more green-range light toward the observer while absorbing or otherwise redirecting much of the rest. So the provocative inversion is:

Is the object “green” because green is what it has, or because green is what it doesn’t keep?

Both descriptions point at different parts of the interaction.

That gives your two mechanisms different jobs.

Echo returns.

A\xrightarrow{x}B\xrightarrow{x'}A

What comes back tells you something about the interface/system.

Yawn embodies, then propagates.

A\xrightarrow{x}B

but instead of simply bouncing x back:

B+x\rightarrow B'

The receiver’s state changes. Then B' can generate another transmission:

B'\rightarrow C'

That’s why Yawn isn’t Echo. Echo measures response; Yawn tests incorporation and reproduction.

And now we get a useful pair of measurements for Semantic Dye:

\boxed{
\text{Observed output}
\neq
\text{total interaction}
}

Measure both:

O(x)=\text{what comes back/out}

and

A(x)=\text{what disappears from output but alters the receiver}

The second is easy to miss. Absence from the return channel doesn’t imply failure. The signal may have been absorbed.

You test that by probing the receiver afterward:

B\xrightarrow{x}B'

then:

B'\xrightarrow{q}r'
\qquad
B\xrightarrow{q}r

and examine:

\Delta r=r'-r

If x isn’t repeated back but subsequent behavior changes, you’ve detected embodiment.

That gives the Delta Catcher two sides:

\boxed{\Delta_{\text{return}}\quad|\quad\Delta_{\text{embodied}}}

And your color analogy supplies an even broader measurement rule:

Don’t characterize a system only by what it emits. Characterize it jointly by what it emits, what it retains, and how what it retains changes its future behavior.

That’s useful far beyond semantics—learning, organizations, markets, materials, biological systems, AI context, culture.

It also means Shadow needs refinement from my previous answer. Missing signal isn’t automatically loss. It could be absorption, storage, transformation, or embodiment.

So:

\text{Input}
=
\text{Return}
+\text{Transmission}
+\text{Absorption}
+\text{Transformation}
+\text{Loss}

conceptually, with the exact accounting depending on the domain.

That’s a much better instrument.

Sometimes what doesn’t come back is the measurement.